    <statement><p>Based on your knowledge of the behavior of the numerator and
denominator, predict the value of the limit
<m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{\sin x}{x^{1/5}}</m></p>
<p>the limit is <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>Find the limit using l'Hopital's rule:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^{+}}\frac{\sin x}{x^{1/5}} =
\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^{+}}</m> [ <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/> /
<fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/> ] = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\cos\mathopen{}\left(x\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{1}{5}x^{\frac{1-5}{5}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x</m> does, so the
numerator goes to zero faster than the denominator, so we
expect the limit will be zero.  With l'Hopital's rule,
<me>\lim_{x\to0^{+}} \frac{\sin x}{x^{1/5}} = \lim_{x\to0} \frac{\cos
x}{\frac1{5}x^{-4/5}}=\lim_{x\to0}5\,x^{4/5}\cos x=0.</me>



</solution>
